   119	Low-level provider adapter modules remain `adapter_only`: their caller owns
   120	admission. Readiness/version probes are classified separately and do not become
   121	review evidence.
   122	
   123	### Provider execution surface classification
   124	
   125	Every production provider spawn site is classified by
   126	`ProviderExecutionSurfaceStatus` (`packages/main/src/core/provider-process-registry.ts`),
   127	and `packages/main/src/tasks/provider-execution-inventory-contract.ts` fails if a
   128	new spawn path appears without one:
   129	
   130	| Status | Meaning |
   131	|---|---|
   132	| `managed` | Executes through ManagedHostRun: durable run identity, supervised process group, absolute deadline, exactly one terminal receipt. |
   133	| `admission_only` | Not managed-executed, but routed through the shared provider admission boundary, so the concurrency cap and slot accounting still apply. |
   134	| `adapter_only` | A low-level adapter module whose *caller* owns admission; the adapter itself makes no gate claim. |
   135	| `readiness_probe` | Version/readiness probing only. Never becomes review evidence. |
   136	| `legacy_unmanaged` | A provider spawn that is neither managed nor admission-routed. **Reserved, and currently empty by contract.** |
   137	
   138	`admission_managed` is not a status value: admission and managed execution are
   139	two separate properties, expressed by `admission_only` (admission without
   140	managed execution) and `managed` (both).
