   202	The authority classifications remain disjoint. A native-host reviewer keeps the
   203	signed ReviewRun plus direct ReviewPass/Finding path only when a registered
   204	host-adapter broker signs the exact closeout invocation. Its Ed25519 attestation
   205	binds Story, agent/lane, ReviewRun, writer session, signed dispatch,
   206	adapter/host identity and a one-shot nonce. Lattice stores the registered public
   207	key and nonce-consumption record, but exposes no issuer command and stores no
   208	broker private key. `dispatch`, `review_run_start`, inherited environment values
   209	and raw provider CLIs cannot mint this authority. The host adapter must inject
   210	the signed attestation over a dedicated descriptor for the exact CLI process;
   211	without that integration, native direct evidence is advisory-only and formal
   212	review must use the managed path.
   213	
   214	Process ancestry is secondary evidence. An unobservable process table, as in a
   215	brokered desktop sandbox, does not negate a valid broker signature. When a full
   216	ancestry is observable, any mixed distinct provider families (Claude→Codex or
   217	Codex→Claude) veto direct evidence even if the nearest ancestor matches. The
   218	one-shot local nonce ledger prevents replay but is not represented as protection
   219	against a same-UID/root attacker capable of replacing trusted configuration or
   220	instrumenting the broker.
   221	
   222	A cross-provider CLI reviewer requires a managed terminal receipt;
   223	cross-provider CLI output without that receipt is advisory-only. An explicit
   224	`degraded-single-host` fallback is local DONE assurance through host-verdict
   225	evidence, not direct or managed REVIEW evidence, and cannot borrow either
   226	reviewer authority path.
   227	
   228	## Operator guidance
   229	
   230	`lattice dispatch` prints two explicit execution modes. A native subagent uses
   231	its host lifecycle. A cross-provider CLI reviewer must use `host_run
   232	--managedReview`; raw `bash + codex/claude/agy` is advisory-only and cannot
   233	satisfy the managed evidence path.
   234	
   235	Poll the same run with `host_run_status --waitMs 30000`. Continue observing the
   236	same run across silent intervals. Cancel only on an explicit operator decision.
   237	Start a replacement only after an authoritative terminal and only when its
   238	cleanup proves replacement safety.
   239	
   240	## Retention and trust boundary
   241	
   242	`lattice doctor` reports worker liveness, provider-progress observability,
   243	deadline/cancel state, residuals, replacement safety and retention. Explicit
   244	`doctor --repair` garbage-collects heavy terminal run directories only beyond
   245	90 days and first revalidates sealed spec, result, event-journal, proposal and
   246	evidence-binding digests, then archives the sealed receipt, evidence-binding
   247	digest, binding projection, provider-authored proposal body, and import-seal
   248	pair (evidence-binding.json + evidence-binding.seal.json) under
   249	`.lattice/runtime/host-run-receipts/`. On recover, the proposal body is
   250	re-digested against the sealed receipt's evidenceProposalDigest, the binding
   251	body is re-digested against the archive's evidenceBindingDigest, and the
   252	archived seal's schemaVersion, runId, and bindingDigest are checked the same
   253	way as the live path. Heavy live-output and result provider text are not
   254	retained. Pre-F-127 archives that lack the later capture fields refuse closed
   255	rather than reconstructing missing bodies. Any mismatch is reported as corrupt
   256	and retained for manual review. GC does not treat a receiptId match as
   257	sufficient: an incomplete leftover is atomically replaced from still-valid
   258	live artifacts, and if neither the existing archive nor the live runtime can
   259	form a complete capture, the live directory is retained. The import-time evidence-binding seal is
   260	checked before GC, and an expired cross-host run removes only its canonical
   261	temporary detached snapshot. Active or corrupt runs and
   262	runs that still contain one-shot inputs are retained fail-closed. Failed startup
   263	removes one-shot secrets immediately.
   264	
   265	The local filesystem boundary does not defend against a same-UID or root
   266	attacker. Retained output may contain sensitive provider text and must be
   267	treated accordingly.
   268	
   269	## Compatibility and non-goals
   270	
   271	- Existing finite provider timeouts, cross-host policy ceilings, bounded retry
   272	  and fail-closed classifications remain in force.
   273	- Ordinary non-review host runs remain compatible but now gain durable
   274	  ownership and shared admission.
   275	- No remote scheduler, daemon, queue or native-host subagent abstraction is
   276	  introduced.
   277	- Usage diagnostics remain non-authoritative and never become lifecycle proof.
